{
  "module": "The transformer block",
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "stack-and-size",
    "title": "Stacking blocks, and how big a block is",
    "summary": "Why a block keeps the shape n × d_model in and out so that blocks can be stacked, and where a model's parameters live: about 12 · d_model² per block.",
    "covers": ["block-stack-shape-contract", "params-per-block"],
    "prerequisites": [
      {
        "lesson": "preln-block",
        "reason": "The shape contract is a property of the block just described."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "multi-head",
        "reason": "The attention share of the parameters, 4 · d_model², comes from there."
      },
      {
        "lesson": "feed-forward",
        "reason": "The feed-forward share of the parameters comes from its two matrices."
      }
    ]
  },
  "screens": [
    {
      "ref": "shape-contract",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "A block maps "
            },
            {
              "text": "n × d_model to n × d_model",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": the same shape in and out."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "It sounds like a small detail, but it is what makes everything else possible."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 61)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "why-it-matters",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Because the shape does not change, the blocks can be "
            },
            {
              "text": "stacked freely",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ": the output of one is a valid input to the next, however many there are. And each block's output can be "
            },
            {
              "text": "added back onto its input",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": ", which is the residual connection."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "This is why the running n × d_model tensor can be viewed as a residual stream that every block reads from and adds to."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 61)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "attention-share",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Now the size. Where do a block's parameters live? First, attention: W_Q, W_K, W_V and W_O are each d_model × d_model, so attention holds "
            },
            {
              "text": "4 · d_model²",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " parameters."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 64)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "ffn-share",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Next the feed-forward sublayer, with d_ff = 4 · d_model. Its two matrices are d_model × d_ff and d_ff × d_model, so together:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "2 \\cdot d_{\\mathrm{model}} \\cdot 4 d_{\\mathrm{model}} = 8\\, d_{\\mathrm{model}}^{2}",
          "spoken": "two times d model times four d model equals eight d model squared"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "For d_model = 512 that is 2,097,152."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 64)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "per-block",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Add the two: 4 · d_model² + 8 · d_model² = "
            },
            {
              "text": "12 · d_model²",
              "bold": true
            },
            {
              "text": " per block. With L blocks, the non-embedding parameters come to about:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "12\\, L\\, d_{\\mathrm{model}}^{2}",
          "spoken": "twelve times L times d model squared"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "For GPT-2 small (L = 12, d_model = 768): 12 × 12 × 768² = 84,934,656, about 85 million."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 64, 65)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "ref": "check",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "Two checks against the deck's figures. GPT-3 has L = 96 and d_model = 12,288: 12 × 96 × 12,288² = 173,946,175,488, about 1.74 × 10¹¹, close to the quoted 175 billion."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "GPT-2 small's embedding table has 50,257 × 768 = 38,597,376 parameters. Add that to the 84.9 million above and you reach 123.5 million, against the 124 million released. The rest is small: position table, norms and biases."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "tone": "note",
          "spans": [
            {
              "text": "These are rules of thumb that ignore biases, norms and other small parts. The point is that almost all of a model's parameters sit in the blocks' matrices."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "Transformers in LLMs: mechanics and pipeline (flashkarte deck, cards 64, 65, 2)"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "questions": [
